Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 07:12:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Normalize absolute robot-count thresholds against local
 population

Per NOTES.md handoff: replace NATURAL_SPREAD_ANOMALY_ROBOTS and
KHOP_SPLIT_CLUSTER_MIN_SIZE (both fixed absolute counts) with ratios
normalized against the locally-observable robot count
(khop_state.last_split_group_size, set from
khop_reachable_robot_count(leader, NATURAL_SPREAD_LOCAL_HOPS)) so these
detection thresholds stay meaningful as cap/witness-pool sizes change,
instead of silently drifting the way NATURAL_SPREAD_ANOMALY_ROBOTS did
when the root cap grew from 29 to ~38 members in an earlier round.

- Added natural_spread_anomaly_threshold() and
  khop_split_cluster_min_size() helpers, each a ratio of
  khop_state.last_split_group_size with a floor.
- Ratios (0.0145, 0.0373) calibrated to reproduce the previously tuned
  absolute thresholds (7, 18) at the population actually observed at
  anomaly detection in this codebase's swarm size (~483 robots).
- KHOP_SPLIT_MIN_GROUP_SIZE is left untouched and documented as an
  intentionally absolute physical/geometric floor (corridor-width-driven
  cap/gate viability, connected-tree re-election, retry pool size) --
  distinct from the detection-evidence use case that used to share its
  value via the now-removed KHOP_SPLIT_CLUSTER_MIN_SIZE alias.
- Scoped to normalization only, per the user's explicit prior decision;
  the unified weighted composite score S_J(t) remains out of scope.

Verification (14000-step re-run of the pre-normalization baseline, and a
7000-step regression run of this change) is in progress in the background
and not yet complete as of this commit -- see NOTES.md for status.

